  <static seed="1931" source="Contrib/PCC/BasicMath/IntegerAndSquareRoot/identifySquareNumbers50.pg">
    <statement><p>Evaluate the following.</p>
<p>Do not use a calculator.</p>
<p><ol label="a.">
<li>
<p><m>\displaystyle{ \sqrt{100} }</m> = <fillin characters="13" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\displaystyle{ \sqrt{10000} }</m> = <fillin characters="13" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\displaystyle{ \sqrt{1000000} }</m> = <fillin characters="13" name="AnSwEr0003"/></p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>       10</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>      100</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>     1000</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>First, recognize that <m>100</m> is a square number. Since <m>10\cdot10=100</m>, we have <m>\sqrt{100}=10</m>.</p>
<p>Next, since <m>100\cdot100=10000</m>, we have <m>\sqrt{10000}=100</m>.</p>
<p>Finally, since <m>1000\cdot1000=1000000</m>, we have <m>\sqrt{1000000}=1000</m>.</p>
</solution>
